WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.780
Know where your business stands before you grow it.

2
00:00:03.130 --> 00:00:08.660
This is the video for module one of Women Entrepreneurship and Small Business Development.

3
00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:14.410
You already run a business. Before you spend on growth, see where it stands today.

4
00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:20.540
You will work out your real profit, and what the business pays you for each hour you give it.

5
00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:24.440
You will name the one thing that is holding you back.

6
00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:28.780
And you will find out who really buys from you, and why.

7
00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:36.970
At the survival stage, money comes in and goes out the same day, and the owner is not sure whether she made a profit.

8
00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:45.470
At the stable stage, sales are fairly regular, she knows her main costs, and the business pays her something every month.

9
00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:52.610
At the growth-ready stage, demand is bigger than she can supply, and she has the records to prove it.

10
00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:58.890
You cannot safely skip a level. A loan taken at survival stage usually feeds the household.

11
00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:04.940
Profit is what is left after all business costs, not the money in your hand at night.

12
00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:08.910
Aisha wrote down every sale and every cost for four weeks.

13
00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:14.050
Her sales for the month came to 191,000 naira.

14
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:22.440
Her costs, for flour, oil, fillings, packs, gas and transport, came to 128,000 naira.

15
00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:26.320
So her profit was 63,000 naira.

16
00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:34.240
Her diary showed 70 hours of work. 63,000 divided by 70 is 900 naira per hour.

17
00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:40.050
She also refused 3 party orders because her single fryer could not cook fast enough.

18
00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:48.240
Each business has a main limit, and each limit needs a different fix. Demand means not enough people are buying.

19
00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:56.890
Capacity means you cannot make or deliver more. That is Aisha. Customers already want more than one fryer can make.

20
00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:01.340
Cash means you cannot buy stock or inputs in advance.

21
00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:07.520
Time means every task waits for you. The fix is delegation and routines.

22
00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:16.910
If your profit per hour is lower than a helper would cost, the business is quietly using your free labour. That is not failure. It is information.

23
00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:25.440
Next, find out who really buys. People are polite. If you ask whether they would buy, most say yes and never buy.

24
00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:33.450
So ask about what they actually did recently. Past behaviour is the best evidence of future behaviour.

25
00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:43.280
Talk to your best regular customers, customers who stopped buying, and people who buy from a competitor. Each group tells you something different.

26
00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:47.190
Aim for 10 to 15 short conversations.

27
00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:54.610
Ask what happened the last time they bought, what was hardest, and why they chose you. Do not pitch or defend.

28
00:02:55.860 --> 00:03:09.790
Write notes the same day and tally the answers that repeat. Blessing, who runs a laundry in Port Harcourt, found 7 of 12 people said late return was their biggest frustration. Only 2 mentioned price.

29
00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:22.600
So her growth idea changed from cut prices to a 48-hour return with office pickup. Your profile names who buys, their problem, why they choose you, and why they leave.

30
00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:31.360
One rule. Ask permission before you save a phone number or quote anyone, and never share customers' numbers or chats.

31
00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:38.800
Now put the whole business on one page with the Business Model Canvas. Circle the two weakest blocks.

32
00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:45.650
Then score the ten readiness statements, from 0 for not true to 2 for fully true. Here are five of them.

33
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:54.810
Titi, who keeps hens near Ibadan, scored 11 out of 20. Her lowest areas were no customer profile and no cash reserve.

34
00:03:55.160 --> 00:04:05.250
Under 8, fix records and separate money first. From 8 to 14, fix your two lowest items. 15 and above, you are probably ready to plan growth.

35
00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:17.600
Remember. Growth spent at survival stage leaks into the household. Profit is sales minus all costs, and dividing it by your hours shows if the business pays you fairly.

36
00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:22.920
Name your main limit. Ask about what people did, and fix your two lowest scores.

37
00:04:24.170 --> 00:04:35.220
Collect one month of sales and costs, even if rough, and work out your profit per hour. Hold five discovery conversations. Then draw your canvas and score your readiness.

38
00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:39.940
Keep them safe for the end of the course. Now continue with the lessons below.
